#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:39
msgid "typed, named: `(i32 0):$y`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:41
msgid "machine operands"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:43
msgid "untyped: `$x`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:44
msgid "typed: `i32:$x`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:46 ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:112
#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:155
#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:208
#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:222
msgid "Semantics:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:48
msgid "A typed operand always adds an operand type check to the matcher."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:50
msgid "There is a trivial type inference system to propagate types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:52
msgid ""
"e.g. You only need to use `i32:$x` once in any pattern of a "
"`GICombinePatFrag` alternative or `GICombineRule`, then all other patterns "
"in that rule/alternative can simply use `$x` (`i32:$x` is redundant)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:57
msgid ""
"A named operand's behavior depends on whether the name has been seen before."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:59
msgid ""
"For match patterns, reusing an operand name checks that the operands are "
"identical (see example 2 below)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:61
msgid ""
"For apply patterns, reusing an operand name simply copies that operand into "
"the new instruction (see example 2 below)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:64
msgid ""
"Operands are ordered just like they would be in a MachineInstr: the defs "
"(outs) come first, then the uses (ins)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:67
msgid ""
"Patterns are generally grouped into another DAG datatype with a dummy "
"operator such as `match`, `apply`, `combine` or `pattern`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:70
msgid ""
"Finally, any DAG datatype in TableGen can be named. This also holds for "
"patterns. e.g. the following is valid: `(G_FOO $root, (i32 0):$cst):$mypat`. "
"This may also be helpful to debug issues. Patterns are *always* named, and "
"if they don't have a name, an \"anonymous\" one is given to them. If you're "
"trying to debug an error related to a MIR pattern, but the error mentions an "
"anonymous pattern, you can try naming your patterns to see exactly where the "
"issue is."
msgstr ""

