#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:480
msgid "GICombinePatFrag"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:482
msgid ""
"`GICombinePatFrag` is an equivalent of `PatFrags` for MIR patterns. They "
"have two main usecases:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:485
msgid ""
"Reduce repetition by creating a `GICombinePatFrag` for common patterns (see "
"example 1)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:487
msgid ""
"Implicitly duplicate a CombineRule for multiple variants of a pattern (see "
"example 2)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:490
msgid "A `GICombinePatFrag` is composed of three elements:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:492
msgid "zero or more `in` (def) parameter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:494
msgid "zero or more `out` parameter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:496
msgid "A list of MIR patterns that can match."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:498
msgid ""
"When a `GICombinePatFrag` is used within a pattern, the pattern is cloned "
"once for each alternative that can match."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:501
msgid "Parameters can have the following types:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:503
msgid "`gi_mo`, which is the implicit default (no type = `gi_mo`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:505
msgid "Refers to any operand of an instruction (register, BB ref, imm, etc.)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:506
msgid "Can be used in both `in` and `out` parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:507
msgid ""
"Users of the PatFrag can only use an operand name for this parameter (e.g. "
"`(my_pat_frag $foo)`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:510
msgid "`root`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:512
msgid "This is identical to `gi_mo`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:513
msgid ""
"Can only be used in `out` parameters to declare the root of the pattern."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:515
msgid "Non-empty `out` parameter lists must always have exactly one `root`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:517
msgid "`gi_imm`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:519
msgid "Refers to an (potentially typed) immediate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:520
msgid "Can only be used in `in` parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:521
msgid ""
"Users of the PatFrag can only use an immediate for this parameter (e.g. "
"`(my_pat_frag 0)` or `(my_pat_frag (i32 0))`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:524
msgid ""
"`out` operands can only be empty if the `GICombinePatFrag` only contains C++ "
"code. If the fragment contains instruction patterns, it has to have at least "
"one `out` operand of type `root`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:528
msgid ""
"`in` operands are less restricted, but there is one important concept to "
"remember: you can pass \"unbound\" operand names, but only if the "
"`GICombinePatFrag` binds it. See example 3 below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../GlobalISel/MIRPatterns.md:532
msgid ""
"`GICombinePatFrag` are used just like any other instructions. Note that the "
"`out` operands are defs, so they come first in the list of operands."
msgstr ""

